# PROJECT:ZED // ANOMALY

A fan site for League of Legends' PROJECT: Zed, built as an in-world archive rather than a normal
fan page. The conceit is that you're browsing PROJECT Corporation's own files on asset ZED and his mission
records, CEDD diagnostics, redacted internal mail, and something that isn't supposed to be in those
files keeps annotating them in amber.

That something is TRIXE, my OC. She started as a behavioural stabiliser installed during Zed's
augmentation. Somewhere she started choosing her own words. The corporation calls that an
emotional artifact and schedules its removal. The story never settles whether what she feels is real
or a very good routine that learned care improves performance retention, and neither does the site.

Live at **trixelle.net**

---

## Why it's built the way it is

The site runs on [Carrd](https://carrd.co), which is a one-page site builder. Carrd handles the
hosting, the page skeleton and the text I edit most often. Everything interactive is dropped in
through Carrd's **Embed** element as raw HTML/CSS/JS.

So the repo is a pile of self-contained code blocks that get
pasted into Carrd embeds, in order. Each one is independent, delete any single block and the rest of
the site keeps working.

You need **Carrd Pro Standard** for this. Embeds and forms aren't available on the free or Lite plans.

---

## The blocks

Paste each file into its own Embed element, Type: Code, Style: Inline, in this order.

| Block | What it does |
|---|---|
| `A1-system` | Design tokens, fonts, HUD markup, nav, the boot gate. CSS and HTML only. |
| `A2-system-script` | The shared animation scheduler, cursor telemetry, neural load, TRIXE's voice channel, boot logic. |
| `B-hero` | Masthead with parallax wordmark. |
| `C-projects` | Chapter index. Expanding dossiers with case codes and metadata. |
| `D-archive` | The CEDD document reader. Five documents, hover a redaction to read it. |
| `T-theatre` | Faction and threat board. Later-act entries stay blurred behind a clearance gate. |
| `U-audit-flag` | Optional. One designated redaction trips an audit banner that TRIXE then withdraws. |
| `E1-identity` | Schematic markup and styles. |
| `E2-identity-script` | Scroll-driven zoom, 1× to 190×, five nested layers. |
| `G-descent-gauge` | Replaces the scrollbar with an altitude readout. Scrolling down is descending the City. |
| `H-cursors` | Custom cursors, base64'd into the CSS so there's nothing to host. |
| `J-cursor-audio` | Cursor follower and the ambient audio, which is synthesised in-browser rather than loaded. |
| `K-purge` | Type `purge` anywhere. Nothing good happens, then she stops it. |
| `L-sigil` | The PROJECT mark as four filled SVG paths, injected into headings and the boot gate. |
| `M-power-on` | CRT turn-on when the boot gate clears. |
| `S-chapter-modal` | The chapter reader. See below. |
| `F1-comms-top` | Comms header and inbound ticker. |
| `N-comms-terminal` | The terminal you can type into. |
| `F2-comms-footer` | Footer. |

A1 has to come first because everything else reads its CSS variables and registers with its
scheduler. Beyond that the order only matters for where things appear on the page.

---

## How chapters work

Chapters are pasted as plain text into ordinary Carrd **Text** elements,
in a section at the bottom of the page with padding set to None. `S-chapter-modal` finds them, hides
the source, and re-renders them into a popup with full formatting.

The first line of each Text element is a marker and has to stay exactly as written:

```
::CHAPTER 1 :: Routine Work :: CL-CE/19-447B :: MID-TIER DISTRICT
```

Everything after it is the chapter, one paragraph per line.

The reader works out the formatting on its own. It reads each paragraph and decides which voice it's
in:

- `[SYNAPTIC LATENCY: 0ms]` — telemetry, grey mono
- `[You are stable.]` — TRIXE, amber with a left rule
- `CASE FILE: CL-CE/19-447B` — mission report, mono on a plate with red corner brackets
- `UPGRADE TO ASCEND` — doctrine billboard, bold, one line per row
- anything else — body prose

The trick for telling telemetry from her: **telemetry never punctuates.** `[END OF LOG]` is the
system. `[YOU ARE STABLE.]` is her, even though it's in caps.

This setup exists because Carrd's code editor has a size ceiling somewhere between 14k and 19k
characters, and chapters run 19k–29k. Pasting them as HTML meant splitting every chapter across two
or three embeds, which was miserable to maintain. Text elements have no such limit. Revising a
chapter is now one paste into one element.

`chapters.py` is the converter that turns the source PDFs into those paste files. It rebuilds
paragraphs from the PDF's hard line wrapping, so the text on the site is character-identical to the
manuscript and I never retype anything.

---

## Things that broke and why

Writing these down because every one of them cost hours and none of them are obvious.

**Unclosed tags nuke everything below them.** An embed missing its closing `</script>` doesn't just
break that embed. The HTML parser doesn't respect embed boundaries, so it swallows the whole rest of
the document as JavaScript and every block after it silently dies. 

**`position: fixed` isn't fixed inside a transform.** Carrd puts transforms on sections for its
entrance animations, and any ancestor with a transform, filter or `contain` makes `fixed` position
against that element instead of the viewport. Every fixed layer here moves itself to `<body>` on load
to get out from under it.

**`z-index` only competes inside its own stacking context.** A `z-index: 9999` overlay nested in a
transformed section still loses to the next section. It stayed visible but stopped receiving clicks,
which looks exactly like a dead button.

**Hiding with `transform` leaves the element clickable.** A banner pushed off-screen with
`translateY(-100%)` is still in the layout, still painted and still eating clicks. Use `display` or
add `pointer-events: none`.

**Class names collide across blocks.** `.rd` was the archive's redaction class and I reused it for
the chapter reader, which repainted every chapter as a solid white bar. Everything is prefixed now.

**Optimizations.** The two biggest performance wins were deleting a `mix-blend-mode`
on a full-screen overlay and a `backdrop-filter` on the modal scrim. Both force the browser to
recomposite the entire page every frame, forever. Reading a chapter went from 46fps to 100fps once
those were gone and the background animations paused behind the modal.

---

## Assets

`assets/` has the processed art — the PROJECT sigil as transparent PNGs, a cutout of the jump
splash with the background and beam keyed out, the wireframe recoloured to red on transparency.
`paste-into-carrd/` has the four chapter text files.

